WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.893
An image is built from a Dockerfile, a list of build steps that Docker follows from top to bottom.

2
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.338
The first step picks a base image.

3
00:00:11.338 --> 00:00:17.988
The slim Python image already brings four layers: Debian files, the Python build, and two small ones.

4
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.282
Each step that changes files adds one read-only layer, on top of the ones below.

5
00:00:24.282 --> 00:00:28.051
A layer holds only the file changes that one step made.

6
00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:33.006
The pip install layer holds the weight, 26.7 megabytes.

7
00:00:33.006 --> 00:00:36.280
The code layer is only 12.3 kilobytes.

8
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.030
Steps that only change settings, like the default command, add no layer.

9
00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:43.169
They only edit the image's config.

10
00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:49.485
Docker names every layer by its digest, a sha256 hash of its content.

11
00:00:49.485 --> 00:00:53.621
The same content gets the same name, on every machine.

12
00:00:53.833 --> 00:00:56.507
Change one byte, and the name changes.

13
00:00:57.633 --> 00:00:59.033
Images share layers.

14
00:00:59.033 --> 00:01:03.513
Build two images from the same base, each adding one small file.

15
00:01:03.733 --> 00:01:07.772
Their four base layers are the same bytes, with the same digests.

16
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.185
So your disk and the registry store them only once.

17
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.199
docker system df listed each image at 178 megabytes.

18
00:01:16.199 --> 00:01:20.352
But only 16.84 kilobytes of each was its own.

19
00:01:22.367 --> 00:01:28.411
Start a container from the nginx image, and Docker adds one thin writable layer on top.

20
00:01:28.633 --> 00:01:32.080
OverlayFS shows all the layers as one folder tree.

21
00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.388
That merged view is the container's root folder.

22
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.833
A read looks from the top layer down, and uses the first copy of the file it finds.

23
00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:46.251
The first write to that file copies the whole file up, into the container layer.

24
00:01:46.251 --> 00:01:48.194
Later writes change that copy.

25
00:01:48.194 --> 00:01:49.620
This is copy-on-write.

26
00:01:49.833 --> 00:01:52.699
A new file goes straight into the container layer.

27
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:57.749
A delete only adds a small marker, a whiteout, that hides the file below.

28
00:01:57.967 --> 00:02:03.733
docker diff lists those changes: C for changed, A for added, and D for deleted.

29
00:02:03.967 --> 00:02:07.283
Remove the container, and its layer goes with it.

30
00:02:07.283 --> 00:02:08.908
The image never changes.

31
00:02:09.733 --> 00:02:10.815
Back to building.

32
00:02:10.815 --> 00:02:14.125
Docker keeps every layer it builds in a build cache.

33
00:02:14.125 --> 00:02:21.636
On the next build, if a step and everything before it are unchanged, Docker reuses the saved layer, and prints CACHED.

34
00:02:22.367 --> 00:02:29.605
But if something changed, Docker rebuilds that layer, and every layer after it, even ones whose steps did not change.

35
00:02:29.833 --> 00:02:30.099
Why?

36
00:02:30.099 --> 00:02:35.422
Each layer holds the changes one step made, on top of the exact layers below it.

37
00:02:35.422 --> 00:02:43.805
If a lower layer changes, Docker cannot know whether a later pip install would give the same result, without running it again.

38
00:02:44.633 --> 00:02:46.764
So the order of the steps matters.

39
00:02:46.764 --> 00:02:50.023
Say you copy all your code first, and install after.

40
00:02:50.233 --> 00:02:52.532
Now edit one comment in your code.

41
00:02:52.532 --> 00:02:57.671
The copy step changes, so the slow install runs again, on every single edit.

42
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:08.199
So order the steps from rarely changed to often changed: the base image, then the requirements file on its own, then pip install, and the code last.

43
00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.092
Now a code edit rebuilds only the last, small layer.

44
00:03:12.092 --> 00:03:18.696
In the lesson, that build took 0.3 seconds, against about 10 seconds for a fresh pip install.

45
00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:23.388
Layers are read-only, named by their content, and shared.

46
00:03:23.388 --> 00:03:26.185
A container adds one thin writable layer.

47
00:03:26.185 --> 00:03:31.232
And a change rebuilds every step after it, so slow, stable steps go first.
